[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the King's Indian"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Why play the King's Indian? It gives Black an unbalanced game with real winning chances against 1.d4. Black lets White build a big center with c4, d4 and e4, tucks the king away behind the bishop on g7, and only then strikes at the center with ...e5 or ...c5. When the center locks, each side attacks on its own wing, and the player who knows the plans has the edge.

Compare 1...d5, the Queen's Gambit. There Black holds the center at once, the pawn structures are often symmetrical, and Black mostly plays to equalize. In the King's Indian the structure is unbalanced from the start, so both sides have targets.

The price is space. White's pawns control more of the board, and Stockfish usually gives White +0.4 to +0.6 in the main lines, a little more than against the solid defenses. That number measures White's long-term edge, not a forced win. In practice Black's counterplay makes White's life hard.

Until the mid-1930s the King's Indian was thought to be dubious. From the late 1930s, Soviet players, above all Isaac Boleslavsky and David Bronstein, showed how much active play Black gets. Bobby Fischer and Garry Kasparov made it famous. Kasparov gave it up after several important losses to Vladimir Kramnik, but Hikaru Nakamura and Teimour Radjabov have kept it alive at the top.

Step through the moves to see the setup.}
1. d4 Nf6 {Stops e4 for now and keeps every option open.}
2. c4 g6 {[%cal Gf8g7] Prepares ...Bg7. The bishop will aim down the long diagonal at d4 and the queenside.}
3. Nc3 Bg7 {This is the King's Indian Defense. 3...d5 instead would be the Grünfeld, a different opening.}
4. e4 d6 {[%cal Gd6e5,Gc7c5] Stops e5, which would chase the knight from f6, and prepares Black's two pawn breaks, ...e5 and ...c5.}
5. Nf3 O-O {The king is safe behind the g7 bishop and the pawns on f7, g6 and h7. White's center looks big, but it is a target.}
6. Be2 Na6 {[%cal Ga6c5,Ge7e5] This is the Kazakh Variation, our answer to the Classical. The knight prepares ...e5 and heads for c5 once White closes the center with d5. It is Stockfish's first choice here (+0.5) and needs far less theory than the main line 6...e5. The same knight maneuver works against the Averbakh and the Makogonov.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for both sides"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/1pp2pbp/3p1np1/p1nPp3/2P1P3/2N2N2/PPQ1BPPP/R1B2RK1 w - - 0 10"]

{[%cal Ga5b4,Gc5e4,Gf6h5,Gf7f5,Rf3d2,Rd2b3,Rb2b4] [%csl Yc5] The Kazakh plan in one picture, after 6.Be2 Na6 7.O-O e5 8.d5 Nc5 9.Qc2 a5 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White). The knight on c5 hits e4, and ...a5 stops b4, so White can't chase it away (green). Next comes ...Nh5 or ...Ne8 and ...f5, Black's break against e4. White tries to dislodge the knight with Nd2 and Nb3, or with b3, a3 and b4 (red), and later to break with c5.

The Classical (5.Nf3 O-O 6.Be2) is the most common system: 5.Nf3 comes up in about a sixth of your games, and 6.Be2 in half of those. Play 6...Na6 and 7...e5. If White closes the center with d5, play ...Nc5, ...a5 and ...f5. If White trades on e5, the queens come off and the position is equal; the knight on a6 guards c7. Against Be3, keep the tension with ...Qe7 or ...Ng4.

6.Bd3: the bishop blocks the queen's guard of d4. Pin the knight with 6...Bg4, take it, and pile up on d4 with ...Nc6 and ...Nd7.

The Makogonov (h3): White stops ...Bg4 and ...Ng4 and may push g4 to prevent ...f5. Play ...e5; after d5, bring the knight to c5 with ...Na6 and ...a5, as in the Kazakh. If White pushes g4, play on the queenside with ...c6 and ...b5.

The Four Pawns Attack (5.f4): White's four center pawns look strong but are loose. Strike at once with ...c5. After d5 e6 and ...exd5 you have a Benoni structure: ...Re8 against e4, ...b5 on the queenside, ...Bg4 to trade the knight that supports e5.

The Sämisch (5.f3 and 6.Be3): White wants Qd2, castling long, h4-h5 and Bh6, the same attack as against the Dragon Sicilian. Hit the center first with 6...c5. After d5, play ...e6 and ...b5; after dxc5 dxc5 and a queen trade, your active pieces make up for the pawn.

5.Be2 with Bg5 (the Averbakh): the bishop pins the knight, so ...e5 at once runs into Nd5. Play ...Na6 first, then ...e5, and unpin with ...Qe8.

5.Bd3 with Nge2: develop with ...Nc6, then ...e5 and ...Nd4.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Pawn structures"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/2p2pb1/1p1p1npp/p1nPp3/2P1P3/2N1B3/PPQNBPPP/R4RK1 b - - 1 12"]

{[%cal Gf6h7,Gf7f5,Gf5f4,Gg6g5,Rc4c5,Rd2b3] [%csl Yc5] Four structures cover most King's Indian middlegames. Learn what each side wants in each one.

The closed center (this position, from the Kazakh). White's pawns on c4, d5 and e4 point at the queenside; Black's on d6 and e5 point at the kingside. Each side attacks where its pawns point. Black moves the f6 knight, plays ...f5, and often follows with ...f4, ...g5 and ...g4 against White's king. White wants c5, prepared with b4 or Nb3. Keep your light-squared bishop: it often joins the attack, sometimes with a sacrifice on h3. With the knight on c5 and the pawn on a5, White's queenside play is much slower than usual.

The open center (...exd4 and Nxd4). Black gives up the center to open the long diagonal and the e-file. The g7 bishop eyes d4 and b2, a rook on e8 presses e4, and the knights head for c5 or e5. White has more space; Black has active pieces and sometimes the break ...d5.

The Exchange (dxe5 dxe5). The center is symmetrical and the d-file is open, so the queens often come off. Each side has a hole: d4 for Black's knights, d5 for White's. With your knight on a6 guarding c7, the endgame is equal, and the knight soon comes to c5.

The Benoni (...c5, d5, ...e6 and ...exd5), from the Four Pawns and the Sämisch. White has a central majority (e4 against d6) and wants to break with e5. Black has a queenside majority and wants ...b5. Stop e5 first: a rook on e8, ...Nbd7 and ...Bg4 to trade the knight that supports it. The g7 bishop, now unblocked, is your best piece.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Type "ideas"]
[FEN "r2q1rk1/pppnppbp/2np2p1/8/2PPP3/2NBBQ1P/PP3PP1/R3K2R w KQ - 3 10"]

{[%cal Gc6d4,Gd7e5,Gg7d4] [%csl Yd4] Six patterns to know.

Hit d4 when White's bishop is on d3. This position comes from 6.Bd3 Bg4 7.h3 Bxf3 8.Qxf3 Nc6 9.Be3 Nd7. Black attacks d4 with the knight and the g7 bishop, and the bishop on d3 blocks the queen's guard of it. The natural 10.Ne2?? loses a piece to 10...Nce5!: after 11.dxe5 Nxe5 the knight forks queen and bishop, and 12.Qg3 Nxd3+ 13.Kf1 Nxb2 leaves Black two pawns up, with the bishop pair against a king that can no longer castle (Stockfish −5.0, winning for Black). 10.Qd1? Nxd4 just wins a pawn (Stockfish −1.3, clearly better for Black). Only 10.d5 keeps the balance.

...Nxe4 and ...Qh4+ against an early Bh6. In the Sämisch after 6.Be3 c5 7.d5 e6 8.Qd2 exd5 9.cxd5 Re8, White's 10.Bh6? walks into 10...Nxe4!. After 11.Nxe4 Qh4+ 12.g3 Qxh6 13.Qxh6 Bxh6, Black has won a pawn and kept a strong bishop (Stockfish −2.1, winning for Black).

The pinned knight on c3. In the Four Pawns, after 6...c5 7.dxc5 Qa5, the queen pins the knight on c3. 8.cxd6? Nxe4! wins back the pawn, because the pinned knight can't take on e4. After 9.Bd2 Nxd6 the material is level, but White's king is stuck in the center and the long diagonal is open (Stockfish −1.2, clearly better for Black). White should play 8.Bd3.

The Nd5 jump, a trap to avoid. In the Averbakh, 6.Bg5 e5? 7.dxe5 dxe5 8.Qxd8 Rxd8 9.Nd5! hits f6 and c7 at once. After 9...Nxd5 10.Bxd8 Black has lost the exchange (Stockfish +1.1, clearly better for White). That is why we play ...Na6 first: the knight guards c7.

White's early e5. If White pushes 5.e5?! after 4...d6, take: 5...dxe5 6.dxe5 Qxd1+ 7.Nxd1 Nfd7, and the e5 pawn has no support (Stockfish −1.1, clearly better for Black).

a3 meets ...a4. In the Kazakh, after 9.Qc2 a5, the move 10.a3?! prepares b4, but 10...a4! fixes White's queenside: if b4, Black takes en passant. The knight on c5 is then safe for good (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Against the London and other second moves"]
[Type "ideas"]

{The course starts after 1.d4 Nf6 2.c4 g6. But White often avoids the King's Indian. After 1.d4 Nf6, Lichess players rated 1800 to 2199 choose 2.c4 in only about half their games. 2.Bf4 (the London) and 2.Nf3 each come up about one game in six, then 2.Nc3 (7%), 2.e3 (5%) and 2.Bg5 (the Trompowsky, 3%).

You don't need new theory for these. Play the same setup, ...g6, ...Bg7, ...O-O and ...d6, then strike with ...e5, or with ...c5. Without a white pawn on c4, ...e5 is even stronger, because White has less space.

The London is the most common. Step through the moves below: the setup takes the sting out of White's bishop on f4, which runs into ...d6 and ...e5. The London System course, as Black, covers this setup in full, with White's other plans (h3 and Be2, or 3.Nc3) and the traps.

2.Nf3: White usually plays c4 next, and you are in the King's Indian. If White plays a London with 3.Bf4, or a quiet e3, use the plan below. Against a fianchetto with g3 and Bg2, play ...Bg7, ...O-O, ...d6, then ...c5 or ...e5.

2.Nc3 g6 3.e4 d6: this is the Pirc Defense, a 1.e4 opening. The same setup works; watch for White's attack with Be3, Qd2 and Bh6, and answer it with ...c6 and ...b5, or by striking in the center with ...e5 or ...d5 (Stockfish +0.5).

2.Bg5, the Trompowsky: 2...g6 is fine. If White takes on f6, recapture with the e-pawn: you get the two bishops, and the position is equal (Stockfish 0.0).}
1. d4 Nf6 2. Bf4 {This is the London System. White plans e3, Nf3, Bd3, c3 and Nbd2.} g6 3. e3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O 5. Bd3 d6 {[%cal Gd6e5] The pawn on d6 stops Ne5 and prepares ...e5. In the Queen's Gambit the London bishop is very active; here it hits a wall.}
6. c3 Nc6 {Supports ...e5. 6...Nbd7 does the same job.}
7. Nbd2 e5 {[%cal Ge5f4,Gd8d3] Black has the initiative, and Stockfish rates the position as equal.

Now the natural 8.dxe5?? loses a piece. After 8...dxe5 the pawn attacks the bishop on f4, and the open d-file lets Black's queen attack the bishop on d3, which the knight on d2 cuts off from its queen. White can save only one of them (Stockfish −6.4, winning for Black). White should keep the tension with 8.Bg5.} *

[Chapter "Classical: 6...Na6"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Kazakh Variation"]
[ECO "E91"]

{The Classical is White's most natural setup: Nf3, Be2 and castling. Our answer is the Kazakh, 6...Na6. The knight supports ...e5, and once White closes the center with d5 it jumps to c5, where it hits e4 and can't easily be driven away. White's seventh- and eighth-move alternatives are under Advanced variations.

White often reaches this position by another move order, with Nf3 before e4. The two short branches show how.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6
3. Nc3
(3. Nf3 Bg7 4. Nc3 {If 4.g3, see the Fianchetto chapter under Advanced variations.} O-O 5. e4 d6 {The same position as the main line after 5.Nf3 O-O. Continue with 6.Be2 Na6 (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).})
3... Bg7
4. e4
(4. Nf3 O-O 5. e4 d6 {The main line again (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White). White's other fifth moves are in the 4.Nf3 chapter under Advanced variations.})
4... d6 5. Nf3 O-O
6. Be2 {Develops and prepares to castle. White keeps every option open.} Na6 {This is the Kazakh Variation. The knight prepares ...e5 and waits for White to push d5, when c5 becomes its square. Compared with the usual 6...e5, it keeps the theory light: the same maneuver works against most of White's systems.}
7. O-O {About two games in three.} e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] The King's Indian strike at the center. This position is also known as the Glek Defense, after 6...e5 7.O-O Na6.

If White takes, 8.dxe5 dxe5, the queens usually come off and the endgame is equal: the knight on a6 guards c7.}
8. d5 {Closes the center, the most common reply.} Nc5 {[%cal Gc5e4] The knight lands on c5 and hits e4 at once.}
9. Qc2 {Defends e4. 9.Nd2 does the same job: answer it with 9...a5 too, then ...Nfd7 or ...Ne8 and ...f5.} a5 {[%cal Ra2a3,Rb2b4] The key move. It stops b4, so the knight on c5 can't be chased away. Without it, White plays b4 and gains space with tempo.}
10. Be3 {The most common move. The bishop eyes the knight on c5.

Other tenth moves: against 10.Bg5, play 10...h6 11.Be3 b6, the same setup. Against 10.a3, answer 10...a4!, which fixes White's queenside: b4 is met by ...axb3 en passant.} b6 {[%cal Gb6c5] Supports the knight. If White takes on c5, Black recaptures with the b-pawn and keeps a solid chain, while White has given up the dark-squared bishop.}
11. Nd2 {Covers e4 and heads for b3 to challenge the knight.} Ng4 {[%cal Gf7f5] Clears the way for the f-pawn and offers to trade the knight for White's bishop.}
12. Bxg4 Bxg4 {Black has the bishop pair, and the light-squared bishop is out.}
13. f3 Bd7 {The bishop steps back. It covers b5 and stays ready to support the kingside.}
14. b3 f5 {[%cal Gf5f4,Gg6g5] Black has carried out the King's Indian plan: ...f5, then ...f4 and ...g5 on the kingside, while the knight on c5 and the pawn on a5 hold White's queenside. The position is equal (Stockfish +0.1).} *

[Chapter "Classical: 6.Bd3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Normal Variation"]
[ECO "E70"]
[Diagram "6.Bd3"]

{After 5.Nf3 O-O, 6.Bd3 is White's second most common move. With the bishop on d3, the queen no longer guards d4, so we aim everything at that pawn. 6.h3, the Makogonov setup, is under Advanced variations.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O
6. Bd3 {[%csl Yd4] Natural, but the bishop blocks the queen's guard of d4.} Bg4 {[%cal Gg4f3] Pins the knight that defends d4. We will take it and pile up on d4.}
7. h3 {Asks the bishop. White's other seventh moves are under Advanced variations. One of them, 7.O-O Nc6 8.h3?, drops the d4 pawn: see the trap chapter.} Bxf3 {Removes the defender.}
8. Qxf3 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4,Gg7d4] Hits d4 with the knight and the bishop.}
9. Be3 {The natural defense. Against 9.d5, jump to d4 anyway: 9...Nd4 10.Qd1 Nd7, with ...c5 next, and the position is equal.} Nd7 {[%cal Gg7d4] Opens the g7 bishop. Now d4 is attacked three times.

White must find 10.d5. The natural 10.Ne2?? loses to 10...Nce5!, and 10.O-O? Nxd4 drops a pawn: both are in the trap chapter.}
10. d5 {The only move that keeps the balance.} Nd4 {The knight jumps forward instead of retreating. No white pawn can attack it on d4.}
11. Qd1 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Supports the knight with a pawn. If White ever takes it, Black recaptures with the c-pawn and keeps a strong pawn on d4.}
12. O-O Rb8 {Prepares ...b5 to open lines on the queenside.}
13. Ne2 e6 {[%cal Ge6d5] Black challenges the d5 pawn. The position is equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Makogonov: 5.h3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Makogonov Variation"]
[ECO "E71"]

{The Makogonov: h3 takes g4 away from Black's knight and bishop, and White may follow with g4 to stop ...f5. Strike with ...e5 as usual. Once White closes the center, the knight goes to c5 via a6, and ...a5 keeps it there. If White pushes g4, switch to the queenside with ...c6 and ...b5.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6
5. h3 {This is the Makogonov Variation.} O-O
6. Nf3 {The most common move. 5.Nf3 O-O 6.h3 reaches the same position.

6.Be3, the Karpov System, is under Advanced variations. Against 6.Bg5, play 6...Na6 first, so the knight guards c7 before ...e5. Against 6.Bd3 or 6.f4, use the plans from the 5.Bd3 and Four Pawns chapters.}
6... e5 7. d5 a5 {[%cal Ga5b4] Stops b4 before the knight comes to c5.}
8. Bd3 {The most common move. Against 8.Be3, 8.Bg5 or 8.Be2 the plan is the same: ...Na6, ...Nc5 and, when the kingside allows it, ...Nh5 and ...f5.} Na6
9. O-O Bd7 {Develops and keeps b5 under control.}
10. Re1 Qe8 {[%cal Gf6h5,Gf7f5] Prepares ...Nh5 and ...f5. The queen also watches a4 and b5.}
11. b3 Nh5 12. a3 f5 {The f-pawn break is in, and Black has active play (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Four Pawns: 6...c5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Four Pawns Attack, Normal Attack"]
[ECO "E77"]

{The Four Pawns Attack: White puts pawns on c4, d4, e4 and f4. The center looks huge, but it is loose, and White is behind in development. Strike at once with ...c5. After d5 and ...e6 the center opens, and Black gets a Benoni structure with active pieces. White's sixth-move alternatives, including 6.e5, are under Advanced variations.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6
5. f4 {This is the Four Pawns Attack.} O-O
6. Nf3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 at once. If 7.dxc5, play 7...Qa5, pinning the knight on c3: 8.cxd6? Nxe4! is a trap (see the trap chapter), and 8.Bd3 Qxc5 gives a normal game (Stockfish +0.3).}
7. d5 {The usual reply: White keeps the center closed.} e6 {[%cal Ge6d5] Undermines d5 and opens the e-file.

Against 8.e5, take with 8...dxe5; against 8.dxe6, recapture 8...fxe6. White's center gives way, and Black is fine.}
8. Be2 {The Normal Attack. 8.Bd3 is under Advanced variations.} exd5
9. cxd5 {White takes back with the c-pawn to keep the pawn on e4. 9.exd5 keeps the pawn chain solid but gives up the grip on e4: play 9...Bf5 and ...Ne4, and the position is equal (Stockfish −0.2).} Re8 {[%cal Re8e4] Attacks e4. White has to decide how to deal with it.

The common 10.Qc2? loses to 10...Nxe4! (see the trap chapter). 10.Nd2 is solid: answer 10...Na6 and ...Nc5, or 10...c4.}
10. e5 {The critical try: White pushes through the center.} dxe5 11. fxe5 Ng4 {[%cal Gg4e5] Attacks e5 at once. If 12.Bf4 or 12.O-O, take on e5 with the knight.}
12. e6 {White gives back a pawn to open lines toward f7.} fxe6 13. d6 {[%csl Yd6] A far-advanced passed pawn, but it needs support.} Nc6 14. O-O Bd7 {Black has developed and kept the extra pawn. The position is roughly equal (Stockfish +0.1).} *

[Chapter "Sämisch: 6...c5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Sämisch Variation, Sämisch Gambit"]
[ECO "E81"]

{The Sämisch: f3 holds e4 and takes g4 away from Black's knight, so White can play Be3 and Qd2 in peace, castle long and storm with h4-h5 and Bh6. Hit the center before that attack gets going: 6...c5. If White takes, Black gives a pawn for quick development; if White closes the center, Black gets a Benoni structure.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6
5. f3 {This is the Sämisch Variation.} O-O
6. Be3 c5 {This is the Sämisch Gambit. White's sixth-move alternatives are under Advanced variations.}
7. d5 {Closes the center, the most common reply. 7.Nge2 and 7.dxc5 are under Advanced variations.} e6 {[%cal Ge6d5] Undermines d5.}
8. Qd2 {Against 8.Bd3, take on d5 and play ...Nbd7 and ...Ne5.} exd5 9. cxd5 Re8 {[%cal Re8e4] Pressure on e4. The natural 10.Bh6? loses a pawn to 10...Nxe4!: see the trap chapter.

Against the calmer 10.Bd3 or 10.Nge2, play 10...Nbd7 and ...Ne5; the knight on e5 is Black's best piece in this structure. Against 10.Bb5, which hits the rook, block with 10...Bd7.}
10. O-O-O?! {The most common move on Lichess, and the one White's plan calls for. But with the center open, the king is safer on the kingside than next to Black's queenside pawns.} Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7e5] Heads for e5 and keeps an eye on c5.}
11. Bh6 {White goes on with the plan: trade the g7 bishop, then h4-h5.} Bh8 {Keeps the bishop. This works because the rook has left f8.}
12. Nge2 Rb8 {[%cal Gb7b5] Prepares ...b5.}
13. Ng3 b5 {[%cal Gb5b4,Gc5c4] The b- and c-pawns open lines at White's king, and ...Qa5 will join in. Black is already much better (Stockfish −2.1, winning for Black).} *

[Chapter "5.Be2: the Averbakh"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Averbakh Variation, Modern Defense"]
[ECO "E73"]
[Diagram "5.Be2"]

{5.Be2 keeps White's options open. 6.Nf3 leads to the Classical, where 6...Na6 is our answer. 6.Bg5 is the Averbakh, which aims to stop ...e5. 6.Be3 is the Semi-Averbakh, which prepares g4.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6
5. Be2 O-O
6. Bg5 {This is the Averbakh Variation. The pin makes ...e5 dangerous: after 6...e5? 7.dxe5 dxe5 8.Qxd8 Rxd8 9.Nd5, White wins the exchange (see the trap chapter).

Against 6.Nf3, play 6...Na6 and you are in the Classical. 6.Be3, the Semi-Averbakh, is under Advanced variations. Against 6.h4, hit the center with 6...c5.}
6... Na6 {Guards c7 in advance. Now ...e5 is safe, because Nd5 no longer hits anything.}
7. Qd2 {Supports the bishop and prepares Bh6 or castling long. Against 7.Nf3, play 7...h6 8.Be3 e5.} e5 {[%cal Ge5d4] Now the strike works.}
8. d5 {If 8.dxe5 dxe5 9.Qxd8 Rxd8 10.Nd5, play 10...Rd6!: c7 is guarded, and the position is equal.} c6 {[%cal Gc6d5] Hits d5 from the side and opens the c-file.}
9. f3 cxd5 10. cxd5 Bd7 {[%cal Ga8c8] Black follows with ...Rc8 and ...Nc5 on the queenside, or ...Qe8 and ...Nh5 on the kingside (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "5.Bd3"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Normal Variation"]
[ECO "E70"]
[Diagram "5.Bd3"]

{5.Bd3 develops the bishop and keeps f2 free; the knight usually goes to e2. Black puts the knight on c6, strikes with ...e5, and plants the knight on d4.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6
5. Bd3 O-O
6. Nge2 {The usual square. 6.Nf3 Bg4 leads to the 6.Bd3 chapter.} Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4,Ge7e5] Hits d4 and prepares ...e5 and ...Nd4.}
7. O-O {Against 7.a3 or 7.f3, play 7...e5 8.d5 Nd4, the same idea.} e5 8. d5 Nd4 {[%csl Gd4] The knight lands on d4, where no white pawn can attack it. If White takes it, Black recaptures with the e-pawn and the g7 bishop's diagonal opens.}
9. Bg5 c5 {Supports the knight with a pawn.}
10. Qd2 a6 {[%cal Gb7b5] Prepares ...b5. Black has a fine game (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Classical: White's seventh moves"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Kazakh Variation"]
[ECO "E91"]
[Diagram "7.a3"]

{White can delay castling. 7.a3 prepares b4 to keep the knight out of c5; 7.Be3 develops first. Against 7.h3, play 7...e5 8.d5 Nc5 9.Qc2 a5, as in the main line.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 Na6
7. a3 {Prepares b4.}
(7. Be3 e5 {If 8.O-O, play 8...Qe7: the same position as 8.Be3 in the previous chapter.} 8. d5 Ng4 {[%cal Gg4e3] Hits the bishop.} 9. Bg5 f6 10. Bh4 g5 11. Bg3 Nh6 {[%cal Gh6f7,Gf6f5] The knight comes back via f7, and ...f5 follows (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).})
7... e5 8. d5 Nc5 9. Qc2 a5 10. Bg5 a4 {[%cal Ga4b3] Fixes the queenside: if b4, Black takes en passant. The knight on c5 is there for good (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Classical: White's eighth moves"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Kazakh Variation"]
[ECO "E91"]
[Diagram "8.dxe5"]

{In the Kazakh, White doesn't have to close the center with 8.d5. 8.dxe5 trades into an equal endgame, and 8.Be3 keeps the tension. Against 8.Bg5, play 8...h6 9.Be3 Ng4; against 8.Re1, 8...Bg4 and ...Nc5 after d5.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 Na6 7. O-O e5
8. dxe5 {Opens the d-file and offers a queen trade.}
(8. Be3 Qe7 {[%cal Ge7e5] Holds e5 and keeps the tension. If White closes the center, ...Ng4 hits the bishop.} 9. d5 Ng4 10. Bg5 f6 11. Bh4 g5 12. Bg3 f5 {[%cal Gf5e4] The kingside pawns roll forward. Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White.})
8... dxe5 {If 9.Nxe5, the trick 9...Nxe4! wins the pawn back: 10.Nxe4 Bxe5, and the position is equal.}
9. Qxd8 Rxd8 10. Bg5 Re8 {[%cal Ge8e5] Unpins the knight and guards e5. The knight on a6 covers c7, so Nd5 does nothing. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Classical: 6.h3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Normal Variation"]
[ECO "E70"]
[Diagram "6.h3"]

{6.h3 is the Makogonov setup with the knight already on f3. It leads straight into the Makogonov chapter. Two rarer sixth moves: against 6.Bg5, the Zinnowitz Variation, ask the bishop with 6...h6; against 6.Be3, the Larsen Variation, hit it with 6...Ng4 (Stockfish +0.2 and +0.3).}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O
6. h3 {Stops ...Bg4 and ...Ng4.} e5 {Strikes at once, as usual.}
7. d5 a5 {The same position as in the Makogonov chapter after 5.h3 O-O 6.Nf3 e5 7.d5 a5. Continue with ...Na6, ...Nc5 and ...f5 (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "6.Bd3: White's seventh moves"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Normal Variation"]
[ECO "E70"]
[Diagram "7.O-O"]

{After 6.Bd3 Bg4, White doesn't have to play h3. Keep the plan: ...Nc6 and ...Nd7 against d4. Against 7.Be2, take on f3 and play ...Nfd7 and ...Nc6. Against 7.d5, play 7...Nbd7 and ...c6.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 Bg4
7. O-O
(7. Be3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 from the side.} 8. d5 e6 9. O-O exd5 10. cxd5 Nbd7 {A Benoni structure with the light-squared bishops still on. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).})
7... Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Hits d4.}
8. Be3 {The best defense. 8.h3? Bxf3 9.Qxf3 Nxd4 drops the pawn (see the trap chapter). Against 8.d5, play 8...Nd4.} Nd7 {[%cal Gg7d4] Opens the bishop, attacking d4 again.}
9. Be2 {The bishop returns, so the queen guards d4.} e5 10. d5 Bxf3 11. Bxf3 Nd4 {The knight settles on d4 (Stockfish +0.2).} *

[Chapter "Makogonov: the Karpov System"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Karpov System"]
[ECO "E71"]
[Diagram "6.Be3"]

{6.Be3 keeps the f-pawn free and prepares g4, Qd2 and castling long. Black plays the usual ...e5 and ...Na6–c5, then opens the queenside, where White's king may go.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. h3 O-O
6. Be3 {This is the Karpov System.} e5 7. d5 Na6 {[%cal Ga6c5] Heads for c5.}
8. g4 Nc5 9. f3 a5 10. Qd2 c6 {[%cal Gc6d5,Gb7b5] With White's pawns on the kingside, Black opens the queenside with ...cxd5 and ...b5 (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Four Pawns: White's other tries"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Four Pawns Attack"]
[ECO "E76"]
[Diagram "6.e5"]

{6.e5 chases the knight at once. It is popular at club level but costs White time and leaves the e5 pawn weak. Against 6.Be2, 6.Bd3 or 6.h3, play 6...c5 as in the main chapter; 6.Be2 c5 7.d5 e6 8.Nf3 exd5 9.cxd5 reaches it exactly.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f4 O-O
6. e5 dxe5 {Takes first. If 7.dxe5, trade queens with 7...Qxd1+ 8.Nxd1 and play 8...Ne4: the e5 pawn is weak (Stockfish −0.6, slightly better for Black).}
7. fxe5 Nfd7 {[%cal Gd7e5,Gg7e5] The knight steps back and attacks e5 with the bishop.}
8. Nf3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Now d4 falls apart too.}
9. e6 fxe6 10. d5 Nf6 {Black is a pawn up with a safe king (Stockfish −0.8, slightly better for Black).} *

[Chapter "Four Pawns: 8.Bd3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Four Pawns Attack, Dynamic Attack"]
[ECO "E76"]
[Diagram "8.Bd3"]

{8.Bd3 covers e4 and prepares castling. The plans stay the same: pressure on e4 and play on the queenside.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f4 O-O 6. Nf3 c5 7. d5 e6
8. Bd3 {Covers e4 and prepares castling.} exd5 9. cxd5 Re8 {[%cal Re8e4] Pressure on e4.}
10. O-O c4 {[%cal Gc4d3] Kicks the bishop off its good diagonal; ...b5 follows.}
11. Bc2 b5 {Black gains queenside space, and the position is level (Stockfish +0.2).} *

[Chapter "Sämisch: White's sixth moves"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Sämisch Variation, Normal Defense"]
[ECO "E81"]
[Diagram "6.Bd3"]

{Whatever White's sixth move in the Sämisch, the answer is 6...c5. After 6.Nge2 c5 7.Be3 you are in the main chapter's 7.Nge2 line.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O
6. Bd3
(6. Bg5 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Qd2 exd5 9. cxd5 a6 {[%cal Gb7b5] Prepares ...b5 (Stockfish +0.2).})
6... c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4.}
7. d5 e6 8. Nge2 exd5 9. cxd5 Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7e5] Heads for e5. Equal (Stockfish 0.0).} *

[Chapter "Sämisch: 7.Nge2 and 7.dxc5"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Sämisch Variation, Sämisch Gambit"]
[ECO "E81"]
[Diagram "7.Nge2"]

{Against the Sämisch Gambit, White doesn't have to close the center at once. 7.Nge2 keeps the tension, and 7.dxc5 accepts the pawn.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 c5
7. Nge2 {Keeps the tension.}
(7. dxc5 {Takes the pawn.} dxc5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. Bxc5 Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4,Gc6b4,Gg7b2] Black is a pawn down, but every piece is active, and White's knight on g1 and bishop on f1 are still asleep. Against 10.Nd5, play 10...Nd7!: after 11.Nxe7+ Nxe7 12.Bxe7 Bxb2 13.Bxd8 Bxa1 the dust settles, and Stockfish rates the position as equal. Against 10.Rd1 or 10.Nge2, play ...Nd7 and ...Nde5 or ...b6. The position is equal (Stockfish 0.0).})
7... Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Adds pressure on d4. Against 8.Qd2, pin the c3 knight with 8...Qa5; after 9.d5 Ne5 the play is like this line.}
8. d5 Ne5 {The knight takes the best square in White's half of the board.}
9. Ng3 e6 10. Be2 exd5 11. cxd5 a6 12. a4 h5 {[%cal Gh5h4] Chases the knight from g3. Black's knight on e5 is excellent (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "5.Be2: the Semi-Averbakh"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Semi-Averbakh System"]
[ECO "E73"]
[Diagram "6.Be3"]

{6.Be3 prepares g4 and Qd2. Black strikes with ...e5 and brings the knight to c5, as in the Kazakh, then hits g4 with ...h5.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O
6. Be3 {This is the Semi-Averbakh System.} e5 7. d5 Na6 {[%cal Ga6c5] Heads for c5.}
8. g4 Nc5 9. f3 a5 10. Qd2 h5 {[%cal Gh5g4] Hits the g4 pawn before White's kingside attack gets going (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Fifth-move sidelines"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Accelerated Averbakh Variation"]
[ECO "E70"]
[Diagram "5.Bg5"]

{Two rarer fifth moves. 5.Bg5 pins the knight early. 5.Be3 usually leads to the Sämisch after 6.f3; against 6.Bd3?!, 6...Ng4! hits the bishop and wins the bishop pair (Stockfish −0.7).}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6
5. Bg5 {This is the Accelerated Averbakh Variation.} O-O {If 6.Be2, play 6...Na6: the Averbakh.}
6. Nf3 {This is the Zinnowitz Variation, reached by another move order.} h6 {Asks the bishop at once.}
7. Be3 Ng4 {[%cal Gg4e3] Hits the bishop again.} 8. Bd2 e5 9. d5 f5 {[%cal Gf5e4] Black has the f-pawn break in early. Equal (Stockfish +0.1).} *

[Chapter "4.Nf3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[ECO "E61"]
[Diagram "5.Bg5"]

{4.Nf3 usually transposes: after 4...O-O 5.e4 d6 you are in the main chapters, and 6.Be2 Na6 is the Kazakh. Against 5.Bf4 or 5.e3, play ...d6 and ...Nbd7 or ...c5. Against 5.g3, play ...d6 and see the Fianchetto chapter.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. Nf3 O-O
5. Bg5 d6 {The pin is less dangerous here than in the Averbakh, because White's pawn is still on e2.}
6. e3 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] With White's pawn on e3 instead of e4, ...c5 is the natural break.}
7. Be2 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 Nh5 {[%cal Gh5g3] Black wins the bishop pair (Stockfish +0.2).} *

[Chapter "Fourth-move sidelines"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense"]
[ECO "E61"]
[Diagram "4.Bg5"]

{4.Bg5, 4.Bf4 and 4.e3 keep the e-pawn back. Castle, play ...d6, and break with ...c5 or ...e5. Against 4.Bf4, ...d6, ...O-O and ...Nbd7 or ...c5 work well; if White plays e4 later, you are back in the main chapters.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7
4. Bg5 O-O 5. e3 d6 6. Nf3 h6 7. Bh4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 now that the e-pawn is on e3.}
8. d5 g5 {Gains space and frees the knight from the pin (Stockfish +0.3).} *

[Chapter "Fianchetto: 3.Nf3 and 4.g3"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Fianchetto Variation"]
[ECO "E60"]
[Diagram "4.g3"]

{In the Fianchetto, White's bishop on g2 meets yours on g7, and the center stays flexible. The plan: ...Nc6 and ...a6, then ...Rb8 and ...b5 on the queenside. When White pushes d5, the knight goes to a5 and ...c5 follows. 3.g3 Bg7 4.Bg2 O-O 5.Nf3 d6 leads to the same position.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7
4. g3 {This is the Fianchetto Variation. If 4.Nc3 O-O 5.e4 d6, you are in the main chapters.} O-O 5. Bg2 d6 6. O-O Nc6 {[%cal Gc6d4] Hits d4.}
7. Nc3 a6 {This is the Panno Variation. Black prepares ...Rb8 and ...b5.}
8. d5 Na5 {[%cal Ga5c4] The knight eyes c4.} 9. Nd2 c5 {[%cal Gb7b5] Black follows with ...Rb8 and ...b5 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} *

[Chapter "Trap: ...Nce5 against 6.Bd3"]
[Diagram "6...Bg4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Normal Variation"]
[ECO "E70"]

{After 6.Bd3 Bg4, everything Black has aims at d4: ...Bxf3 removes its defender, ...Nc6 and ...Nd7 bring two more attackers. White's natural moves often let it go. The worst of them, 10.Ne2??, is White's most common move in the main line on Lichess, played in almost half the games. The only good answer there is 10.d5 (see the 6.Bd3 chapter).}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Bd3 Bg4
7. h3
(7. O-O Nc6 8. h3? {Natural, but it lets Black remove the knight that guards d4.} Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nxd4 {[%cal Gd4f3] The pawn falls, and the knight is safe on d4.} 10. Qd1 e5 {Black is a clean pawn up (Stockfish −1.4, clearly better for Black).})
7... Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nc6 9. Be3 Nd7 {[%cal Gc6d4,Gg7d4] Now d4 is attacked three times.}
10. Ne2?? {Adds a third defender of d4, but the queen and the bishop on d3 are both on squares a knight can fork from e5.}
(10. O-O? Nxd4 11. Qd1 e5 {Black has won the d4 pawn for nothing (Stockfish −1.3, clearly better for Black).})
10... Nce5! {[%cal Ge5f3,Ge5d3] Forks the queen and the bishop. White can take, but the other knight forks them again.}
11. dxe5 Nxe5 {[%cal Ge5f3,Ge5d3] The second fork.}
12. Qg3 Nxd3+ 13. Kf1 Nxb2 {Black has two extra pawns and the bishop pair, and White's king can no longer castle (Stockfish −5.0, winning for Black).} *

[Chapter "Trap: ...Nxe4 in the Four Pawns"]
[Diagram "6...c5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Four Pawns Attack, Normal Attack"]
[ECO "E77"]

{In the Four Pawns, White's e4 pawn is often short of defenders. Two tricks with ...Nxe4 punish natural moves. In the main line, 10.Qc2? is White's choice in about four games in ten on Lichess.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f4 O-O 6. Nf3 c5
7. d5
(7. dxc5 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] Pins the knight on c3 to the king. White should play 8.Bd3.} 8. cxd6? Nxe4! {The pinned knight can't take back.} 9. Bd2 Nxd6 {Material is level again, but White's king is stuck in the center and the long diagonal is open (Stockfish −1.2, clearly better for Black).})
7... e6 8. Be2 exd5 9. cxd5 Re8 {[%cal Re8e4] Pressure on e4.}
10. Qc2? {Natural, but the queen walks onto the same diagonal as Black's bishop.} Nxe4! {Gives the knight for the pawn, for a moment.}
11. Nxe4 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5e4,Re4c2] The knight on e4 is pinned to the queen: if it moves, ...Bxc2.}
12. Nfd2 {The best defense: a second guard for e4.} Na6! {[%cal Ga6b4,Gb4c2] The knight heads for b4 to chase the queen off the e4 knight's defense. Black wins the knight back and ends up a pawn ahead with the better game (Stockfish −2.6, winning for Black).} *

[Chapter "Trap: Bh6? in the Sämisch"]
[Diagram "9...Re8"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Sämisch Variation, Sämisch Gambit"]
[ECO "E81"]

{In the Sämisch, White dreams of Bh6 to trade Black's best defender, followed by h4-h5. Played too early, Bh6 leaves e4 and the queen on d2 exposed to ...Nxe4 and ...Qh4+. Calmer moves such as 10.Bd3 or 10.Nge2 keep White's position together (see the Sämisch chapter).}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Qd2 exd5 9. cxd5 Re8 {[%cal Re8e4] Pressure on e4.}
10. Bh6? {White goes for the attack and forgets about e4.} Nxe4! {Hits the queen on d2.}
11. Nxe4
(11. fxe4? Qh4+ 12. g3 Qxh6 13. Qxh6 Bxh6 {Again Black has won a pawn, and White's broken center leaves Black much better (Stockfish −4.1, winning for Black).})
11... Qh4+ {[%cal Gh4e1,Gh4h6] A double attack: check, and the bishop on h6 is hit.}
12. g3 Qxh6 13. Qxh6 Bxh6 {Black has won a pawn and keeps a strong bishop (Stockfish −2.1, winning for Black).} *

[Chapter "Trap to avoid: ...e5 against the Averbakh"]
[Diagram "6.Bg5"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Averbakh Variation, Modern Defense"]
[ECO "E73"]

{The Averbakh's point: with White's bishop on g5, the usual ...e5 at once loses material, because Nd5 hits the pinned knight on f6 and the c7 pawn together. Play ...Na6 first. Then the knight guards c7, and ...e5 is safe.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O
6. Bg5 Na6!
(6... e5? {The natural strike, but here it doesn't work.} 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. Nd5! {[%cal Gd5f6,Gd5c7] Hits f6 and c7. Because of the pin, Black can't keep both.} Nxd5 10. Bxd8 Nf4 {Black has lost the exchange (Stockfish +1.1, clearly better for White).})
7. Qd2 e5 8. dxe5 dxe5 9. Qxd8 Rxd8 10. Nd5 Rd6! {[%cal Ga6c7] Now c7 is guarded, so the knight jump hits nothing.}
11. f3 c6 12. Ne7+ Kf8 13. Nxc8 Rxc8 {Equal (Stockfish −0.2).} *
